  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 138 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThe upper high will continue its trek westward on Friday, centering \nitself somewhere over western NM. This will allow storm outflows from \nmore robust convection over SE Colorado Thursday night to push \nmoisture south and westward into eastern and central NM Friday. \nStorms will therefore focus over these areas on Saturday when rain \nchances in the eastern plains will be higher than they have been for \ndays. The storms and associated clouds should keep temperatures down \na few degrees, but it will still be a hot day with high temps in the \nupper 90s and low 100s.\n\nThe upper high will likely change location this weekend into early \nnext week, but its exact location is slightly different in the 3 \nensemble means. Regardless of the exact location of the high, slow \nand erratic storm motions are favored, which will elevate the threat \nof flash flooding. PWATs will generally be 100-130% of normal, which \nis notable for late July because it is near the climatological peak \nin atmospheric moisture content. Model guidance continues to diverge \nearly next week, with regard to the location of the high, with the \nlocation ranging from northeast Arizona to the TX Panhandle.",
